# C++ RWE Note — `const` and Pointers

## `const` as Read-Only Privilege

A useful working mental model:

> **`const` means read-only privilege.**

It allows access to something while restricting modification through that particular access path.

### Reference example

```cpp
const std::string& getIdentity() const;
```

There are two separate `const` rules here:

```cpp
const std::string&
```

The caller receives a reference to the existing string, but cannot modify the string through that reference.

```cpp
getIdentity() const
```

The getter promises that calling it will not modify the `Video` object.

## Pointers

The placement of `const` matters with pointers.

```cpp
const int* p;
```

**Pointer to const data.**

You can change where `p` points, but you cannot modify the integer through `p`.

```cpp
int* const p = &x;
```

**Const pointer.**

`p` cannot point somewhere else, but you can modify the integer through `p`.

```cpp
const int* const p = &x;
```

**Const pointer to const data.**

You cannot change where `p` points, and you cannot modify the data through `p`.

### Mental model

```text
const DATA
    ↓
No modification privilege over the data.

const POINTER
    ↓
No modification privilege over where the pointer points.

const DATA + const POINTER
    ↓
Neither can be modified through that pointer.
```

The important lesson is not merely memorizing the syntax.

**Look at what `const` is attached to.**

That tells you what the read-only restriction applies to.


# Shell Scripts — The Basic Idea

A shell script is basically a **small program for the command line**.

Instead of manually typing a long sequence of terminal commands, we put those commands into a file such as:

```bash
build.sh
```

Then we make the file executable:

```bash
chmod +x build.sh
```

and run it:

```bash
./build.sh
```

## Arguments

A script can receive information when we call it:

```bash
./build.sh test
```

Inside the script, the first argument is available as:

```bash
$1
```

So the script can make a condition:

```bash
if [ "$1" = "test" ]; then
    # run test build commands
fi
```

The basic idea is:

```text
build.sh
   ↓
read instructions
   ↓
receive arguments
   ↓
make decisions
   ↓
run commands
```

## Important distinction

The filesystem is case-sensitive:

```bash
Tests
```

and

```bash
tests
```

are different names.

But `test` in:

```bash
./build.sh test
```

is simply an argument we chose to give the script. It does not have to match the directory name.

For example:

```bash
./build.sh test
```

can mean:

> "Build the tests."

while the actual directory remains:

```text
Tests/
```

## Why this matters

We can turn repetitive development commands into our own tools.

Instead of repeatedly typing:

```bash
g++ -I Video Tests/test_video.cpp Video/video.cpp -o test_video
```

we can eventually just type:

```bash
./build.sh test
```

The script becomes a small layer of automation that **we understand and control**.

**C++ builds the application.
Shell scripts build and control the development workflow.**


